# K2 Market Data Platform — Environment Variables
#
# Usage:
#   cp .env.example .env
#   # Edit .env with your values — never commit .env to git
#   # Every change-me-in-production value is a placeholder: replace each with
#   # `openssl rand -hex 24` (LAKEKEEPER_ENCRYPTION_KEY: see its own note).
#   # `make up` refuses to start while one is left (scripts/check-env.py).
#
# .env is loaded automatically by docker-compose from the project root.
# The Python jobs in docker/lake/ read these via os.environ at runtime.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ClickHouse
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Password for the ClickHouse 'default' user (set at container init time).
# Changing this after the container is first created requires recreating the
# container and its volume.
CLICKHOUSE_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production
# Read-only research user on the gold database (docker/clickhouse/users.xml)
K2_QUANT_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PostgreSQL (Airflow + Lakekeeper metadata). The service, role, database and
# PREFECT_DB_* names are historical: kept from before Airflow, ADR-034 says why
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PREFECT_DB_USER=prefect
PREFECT_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production
PREFECT_DB_NAME=prefect

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MinIO (S3-compatible object storage backing the Iceberg lake)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are also used by the Spark container as AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID /
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY so they must match between the two services.
# The user is the S3 access key: it is not a secret (Trino's log printed it
# until 2026-11-26), so the password must never equal it. Generate one:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 24
#
# `make up` and scripts/dev-up.sh refuse an .env whose MinIO user equals its
# password, or any password, secret or key left at the value below, equal to
# its user, a known default or under 16 characters (scripts/check-env.py).
# On a fresh clone set both before the first
# `make up`. On a stack that holds data, editing them here is not enough:
# lake-init stored the pair in each Lakekeeper warehouse at creation.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=k2-minio-root
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lakekeeper (Iceberg REST catalog — v3, ADR-018)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Encrypts warehouse storage credentials at rest in the `lakekeeper` PostgreSQL
# database (LAKEKEEPER__PG_ENCRYPTION_KEY). Lakekeeper documents no minimum
# length, only that the default must not be used in production. Generate one:
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Changing it after warehouses exist makes their stored credentials
# undecryptable — rotate by recreating the warehouse, not by editing this line.
LAKEKEEPER_ENCRYPTION_KEY=change-me-in-production

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Airflow 3 (ADR-034): the lake schedules
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The `airflow` role on prefect-db. docker/airflow/init-db.sh creates it and
# sets this password on every airflow-migrate run, so to rotate it, edit it
# here and run `docker compose up -d --force-recreate airflow-migrate` again.
AIRFLOW_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production
# Shared by the scheduler, the api-server and the dag-processor, and must be
# the same in all three. Generate each with: openssl rand -hex 32
AIRFLOW_JWT_SECRET=change-me-in-production
AIRFLOW_API_SECRET_KEY=change-me-in-production
# The group that owns /var/run/docker.sock inside the scheduler: 0 on Docker
# Desktop, the `docker` group on a Linux engine (`stat -c %g /var/run/docker.sock`).
K2_DOCKER_GID=0

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Grafana
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
GRAFANA_PASSWORD=change-me-in-production

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rust capture tier (v3 Phase C — capture-binance/kraken/coinbase)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CPU cores the capture-* containers (and, disjointly, the heavy tier:
# Spark, Trino and the paused ClickHouse) are pinned to. Shipped EMPTY = no pinning, which
# is the only value that starts on every host: a cpuset naming a core the host
# does not have fails the container with `invalid argument`. Empty wins over the
# compose default because compose uses `${K2_CAPTURE_CPUSET-12-14}` (`-`, not
# `:-`), which only falls back when the variable is unset, not when it is empty.
#
# The 15-core layout below is what
# docs/plans/2026-08-26-v3-quant-research-platform/005-phase-f-notebooks-numbers-docs.md
# specifies — capture three cores away from the low cores ClickHouse and Spark
# contend for. Uncomment it on a host with at least 15 cores, or set your own
# ranges (e.g. `2-3` / `0-1`). Host-specific, which is why it lives here.
#
#     K2_CAPTURE_CPUSET=12-14
#     K2_HEAVY_CPUSET=0-11
K2_CAPTURE_CPUSET=
K2_HEAVY_CPUSET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Host ports
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every published port binds 127.0.0.1 (docker-compose.yml header); these move
# the port, never the interface.
# Airflow's published port (loopback only), one variable because the compose
# file uses it twice: the `ports` mapping and AIRFLOW__API__BASE_URL. 8080 is redpanda-console's.
K2_AIRFLOW_PORT=8090
# Trino's published port: HTTPS with a password login (ADR-060), loopback only all
# the same. 8443 is also its port on the network.
K2_TRINO_PORT=8443

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Releases and local secrets (phase 001, target: docs/development/phase-001-interfaces.md)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A commented line is one nothing in the running stack reads yet: scripts/check-env.py
# would refuse `make up` on an unset secret. The step named on each line uncomments it
# when it makes the value real.
#
# The releases root, outside any checkout; `make deploy` creates it once and every
# service mounts it read-only at /k2/releases (step S19, S22).
#     K2_RELEASES=$HOME/k2-releases
# The live checkout's absolute path, the one compose project directory `make deploy` runs from.
K2_HOME=
# The project the running containers carry: docker inspect k2-trino --format '{{index .Config.Labels "com.docker.compose.project"}}'
K2_COMPOSE_PROJECT=
# Local secrets, outside any checkout, never in git or a release (.gitignore keeps
# a stray copy out): trino/password.db, api/keys.json, principals/<id>.password
# (mode 600), tls/ (`make tls`), alertmanager/slack-webhook-url, canary/. Each
# file, its read-only path in a container and the step that writes it are in one
# table: docs/development/phase-001-interfaces.md (steps S09, S13, S14, S19).
#     K2_SECRETS=$HOME/k2-secrets
# Trino's internal shared secret, with which a holder can pose as a Trino node: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
K2_TRINO_SHARED_SECRET=change-me-in-production
# One storage key pair per engine, replacing MINIO_ROOT_* for everything but
# lake-init (phase 002): Spark read-write on k2; Trino read-write on the domain
# warehouses and read on k2; backup; tools read only.
#     K2_SPARK_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#     K2_SPARK_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# Trino's pair: MinIO's root user and password until phase 002 makes its scoped key.
K2_TRINO_S3_ACCESS_KEY=k2-minio-root
K2_TRINO_S3_SECRET_KEY=change-me-in-production
#     K2_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#     K2_BACKUP_S3_SECRET_KEY=
#     K2_TOOLS_S3_ACCESS_KEY=
#     K2_TOOLS_S3_SECRET_KEY=
# Never set here: the dbt wrapper's environment, passed per run by name to
# dbt-runner (phase 002): K2_TRINO_HOST, K2_TRINO_INTERNAL_PORT (Trino's port on
# the network, 8443; K2_TRINO_PORT above is the host's), K2_TRINO_USER (the
# builder dbt_<fence>), K2_PUB_USER (the publisher pub_<fence>), K2_RUNS_USER
# (svc.runs), K2_CATALOG_URI, K2_RELEASE. Their passwords are files mounted
# read-only at /run/k2-secrets/{builder,publisher,runs}.password, read by the
# wrapper only.
